# 0001. Hexagonal core with import boundaries enforced by a test

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-11

Accepted by the owner on 2026-11-11: business rules sit in a stdlib-only core behind ports,
adapters plug in from outside, and a test fails the build on any forbidden import.

## Technical details
- **Problem**: the platform talks to several replaceable technologies (RunPod, vLLM,
  PostgreSQL, Telegram, HTTP). Business rules (deployment lifecycle, idle scheduling, cost
  accounting) must stay testable without any of them.
- **Layers** (ARCHITECTURE.md §1):
  - `core/domain/{compute,deployment,inference,model,usage}`: types and rules, stdlib only,
    no I/O.
  - `core/ports`: interfaces the core needs (`clock`, `compute`, `events`, `inference`,
    `persistence`).
  - `core/application/{deployment,inference,model,scheduling}`: use cases orchestrating
    domain and ports.
  - `adapters/inbound/{rest,telegram,cli}` drive the core; `adapters/outbound/{compute/runpod,
    inference/vllm,persistence/postgres,messaging}` implement ports.
  - `cmd/{server,telegram,cli}`: wiring only; the only place that reads `config/`.
- **Enforcement**: `internal/archtest/boundaries_test.go` runs `go list` on every package and
  checks each import against a rule table (module-relative prefixes):
  - `core/domain` → only `core/domain`; `core/ports` → `core/domain`;
    `core/application` → domain, ports, application; none of them third-party.
  - `adapters/inbound` → core + `internal`; `adapters/outbound` → domain, ports, `internal`
    (not `core/application`); both may use third-party modules.
  - `config` → `core/domain`; `internal/apitypes` → nothing; `internal/apiclient` →
    `apitypes`; `internal/testing` → domain, ports.
  - Nothing imports `cmd/`. A package may always import its own subtree. Stdlib is always
    allowed.
  It runs in `make check` (`go test -race`), so CI fails on a violation.
- **Fakes**: `internal/testing/fakes` has a fake per port, so orchestration and failure paths
  are unit-tested without network or database.
- **Consequences**: a vendor swap (another GPU cloud, another runtime) touches one adapter;
  the core is tested without I/O. Cost: more packages and mapping code than a flat service,
  and rules a newcomer must learn.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: Go 1.25 (`go list`, `testing`).
- Alternatives: layered packages without enforcement: drifts silently; depguard or another
  linter: an extra CI tool for what one test does.
